WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.045
The threat intelligence lifecycle is a practical way to keep CTI work connected to

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:04.795
decisions.

00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:08.610
The stages are requirements, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.335
and feedback.

00:00:09.335 --> 00:00:12.020
In real teams the flow is not always perfectly linear,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.850
but the model is useful because it reminds analysts that intelligence starts with a need

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.450
and ends with whether the consumer could use the product.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.710
Requirements come first.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.410
An intelligence requirement is a clear statement of what the consumer needs to know in

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.440
order to make a decision or take action.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.710
Vague questions create noisy intelligence.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.880
"Tell me about ransomware" is too broad for most operational work.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.695
Better requirements might ask which ransomware behaviors are relevant to the company's

00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:35.600
technology stack,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.225
which exposed services are being targeted in the sector,

00:00:38.225 --> 00:00:40.835
or which detections should be prioritized this week.

00:00:40.835 --> 00:00:43.780
Requirements also keep the program honest about tradeoffs.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.885
CTI teams rarely have unlimited time.

00:00:45.885 --> 00:00:48.020
If analysts collect broadly without requirements,

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.870
they spend effort sorting data that may never support a decision.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.620
If they define requirements clearly, they can explain why they chose certain sources,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.780
why they ignored others, and what level of confidence is realistic.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.020
Requirements turn CTI from general monitoring into a service that answers defensible

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.010
security questions.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.730
Collection follows the requirement.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.780
The analyst chooses sources that can plausibly answer the question: internal telemetry,

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.720
incident reports, vendor reporting, vulnerability advisories, government notices,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.690
community sharing,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.240
malware reports, open-source research, or threat research blogs.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.068
Collection discipline matters because every source adds cost.

00:01:20.068 --> 00:01:24.390
More sources can mean more duplication, more false positives, more licensing concerns,

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.855
and more review work if the team has not defined what it is trying to learn.

00:01:27.855 --> 00:01:30.725
Processing turns collected material into a usable form.

00:01:30.725 --> 00:01:33.895
That may mean deduplicating indicators, normalizing timestamps,

00:01:33.895 --> 00:01:37.880
extracting affected products, mapping behavior to ATT&CK techniques,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.690
translating a long report into key claims, or separating confirmed facts from speculation.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.560
Analysis then turns processed material into a judgment.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.800
The analyst evaluates source reliability, corroboration, recency,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.020
relevance to the local environment, assumptions, and confidence.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.300
Dissemination delivers the product in the format the audience can act on: a brief, ticket,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:00.420
detection story, watchlist, executive summary, or incident response note.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.730
Feedback closes the loop.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.490
Did the SOC use the triage guidance?

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.925
Did the detection find anything?

00:02:04.925 --> 00:02:06.520
Did leadership understand the risk?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.980
Did the vulnerability team need different context?

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.500
Feedback prevents CTI from becoming a publishing function and keeps it tied to real

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.500
defensive needs.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.950
The lifecycle also gives teams a way to improve.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.020
If a product was ignored, the problem may have been the requirement, the source choice,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.000
the analysis, the format, or the timing.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.750
Reviewing each stage turns misses into process improvements rather than blame.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.110
That is how a CTI function matures from ad hoc reporting into a repeatable service.
